Z||000001||ray b||12-15-2001||03:25 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>wife wants a scanner for film/slides are they realy alot better than flatbeds adapter for slide now?????<BR>DPI is that most important or is the film/slide unit bettter than any adapter for a flatbed could be???<BR>weired idea can you project a slide at/on a flat bed instead of useing a adapter as both shine a lite at the bed with out the scanners underneath lite being on??<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>bump<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||Mark Matthews||12-15-2001||04:18 PM||||If you can afford it get a film scanner.  As long as that's the only thing you'll be scanning.  Flatbeds are good, but if you wanna scan in pictures with high resolution, no flatbed will ever beat a slide scanner.  The one we have at work does 3200dpi.  Most flatbeds will say they can do like 800 or 1600, but that's a bit of a false statement.  Most can only scan at 400 then the scanning software will adjust the res to the higher number.  Expect slower scanning times tho at like 3200, just cuz the slide is small doesn't mean it will be fast.<P>HTH<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top||||reg||1
Z||000003||Patrick's Dad||12-15-2001||04:28 PM||||Ray,<P>Film scanners are still superior to beds for the following:<P>1: You're scanning the original. A print is a second generation copy. A print from a slide is a third gen, unless it is an Ilfochrome or Type R. Regardless, prints are subject to the printer's opinions of color. Negs and slides are truer to the original scene, less filtration.<P>2: Flatbeds' pickup needs to be perfectly aligned to properly scan a print (or anything else). This sort of precision can't be found of a $500 bed, but a $500 film scanner will have an LED transmission pickup that is permanently aligned Within service specs).<P>3: Most film scanner maufacturers are film and camera manufacturers. Their reputation is the image. Electronics companies' is the bottom line. I own a UMAX Astra 1220 because it was cheap and I don't use it for prints that I would sell. It is convenient, and good enough for sending pics of my kids to people out of state. Besides, even when employed, I couldn't afford an Imacon ($11,000+) If you're asking this question, then your wife is serious about her images.<P>4: The newer film scanners are in the neighborhood of 4800+ dpi. These will produce well large enough files with decent color depth (Or gray scale for B&W). This meets or exceeds the better flatbeds (maybe not the pro units).<P>Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000004||ray b||12-15-2001||06:00 PM||||we have a UNMAX 1200S rated at 600x1200 claimed to work at 9600x int rate but it is unused no slot for a scsi card in old computer[v5500 in its place] and no adapter for slides anyway for it.<BR>new 4800 dpi flatbeds USB are cheap and come with adapters for slides at under $200 and pure slide units are way over $500 for lower dpi rated units<BR>$11,000 is more than I want to think about any good ones under 1100 or so???<BR>any thoughts on projector at flatbed IDEA???<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000005||Patrick's Dad||12-15-2001||11:21 PM||||The Imacon scanner thing is just me... champagne taste on a beer budget. It will also scan large format, which I occasionally shoot.<P>Good film scanners can be had by Minolta or Nikon in your price range. Canon is OK, but the color management of the Min/Nik's are better.||||reg||1
Z||000006||artherd||12-16-2001||06:37 AM||||Nikon (coolpix?) 2000 looks great in that budget range. <P>I've got a Leaf 45 here, will scan up to 4x5" large format at insane dpi, and with an acual useable differentiation in excess of 12bits/component. (for *serious* production film effects work.)<P>The difference is night and day, flatbeds can NOT scan film with any sembelance of quality.<P>Best!<BR>Ben<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
